issue_context.body:
Looking at #493 it seems there's a precedent for running into this.

Some specific scenarios that I can't accomplish without deeper access today are:

- shared reference schemas
  - i.e., [this gist](https://gist.github.com/dougrich/d104baa418b88582c85a8b02f49bb6c6) shows a schema with shared references and how we can use both AJV and fast-json-stringify with it
  - I can achieve this today by manually resolving references before passing it into fastify
- re-used schemas
  - schemas that are re-used seem to be recompiled
  - I can achieve this today be creating a second AJV instance and providing our own `schemaCompiler` that provides cached schemas

The closest I can get is to create our own AJV, separate to the one instantiated in fastify, and provide our own `schemaCompiler` that does a `getSchema`, and serializing manually using our own schemas compiled with `fast-json-stringify`.

What would be ideal is:

```js
const fastify = require('fastify')();

fastify.addSchema(require('../schema/api.json')

const opts = {
  schemaId: 'api.json#/definitions/instance:get'
}

// under the hood this does a getSchema call
fastify.get('/case1', opts, function (req, reply) {
  ...
})

// since this is also doing a getSchema call, it re-uses the existing compiled schema in AJV
fastify.get('/case2', opts, function (req, reply) {
  ...
})

// this schema uses the same definitions as the instance:get schema
const putOpts = {
  schemaId: 'api.json#/definitions/instance:upsert'
}

fastify.put('/case1', putOpts, function (req, reply) {
  ...
})
```

Alternatively, this could be a third party library, but it feels like it would duplicate a large part of fastify's validation and serialization logic.

Thoughts?

P.S.

In your [code of conduct](https://github.com/fastify/fastify/blob/master/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md) you've still got an [INSERT EMAIL ADDRESS] placeholder in the Enforcement section.

I'm also a big fan of fastify - I really like the focus on performance. Thank you for all your hard work on this.

