@delvedor Thanks! I had looked at [any-schema-you-like](https://github.com/fastify/any-schema-you-like) - it's what I was looking at when I was thinking about how to approach this as a plugin for fastify. 😄 

But it seemed like it's only for the reply serializer and it doesn't appear to support external schema references. Would it be better to extend that library with support for request validation and external references or to add the `addSchema` approach as above?

I'll take a look at what would be involved with the `addSchema` approach, which will be useful in either library.

Yes, there is some power and features of Ajv that we can't use, without adding `ajv` to our package dependencies and using `fastify.setSchemaCompiler` and doing some things ourselves, which isn't the end of the world, but it would be nice if we could avoid it.

Besides `addSchema` functionality, I think the [`addKeyword` functionality](https://github.com/epoberezkin/ajv#addkeywordstring-keyword-object-definition---ajv) is also very important, if you are validating content and not just shape/structure. It is not currently possible to use Ajv's support for custom keywords without adding `ajv` to our package dependencies, etc..

Here is the API I proposed in PR #495 (which includes a test similar to this example):

```
const fastify = Fastify({
  ajv: (Ajv, defaultOptions) => {
    return new Ajv(defaultOptions)
      .addSchema(require('./example-schema-set.json'));
  }
})
```

(This in addition to accepting an object for the `ajv` option, of course)

That said, I'm guessing first-class support for `addSchema` (i.e. `fastify.addSchema`) would be way better than apps configuring Ajv in this way themselves directly.
